course: everyday
lesson: 444
title: «Free skins» and scams in games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sam saw an ad: «FREE SKINS! Click here, log in with Roblox.» The page looked just like Roblox. He typed his username and password. Next morning his account was gone — two years of items, sold to a stranger. He and his mum wrote to Roblox, but nothing came back. Sam felt sick and stupid for a week. His mum said the shame belongs to the scammer, not to him, and that real game companies never ask for your password on another website.
explain: Scammers often pretend to have power. A player told Priya, «I'm a mod (a moderator, a helper who runs the game chat), give me your password so I can verify your account.» Her dad said «Real mods never ask for passwords. Block and report.» In Fortnite someone offered Jayden in Atlanta «double V-bucks» if he sent his gift card code first. Jayden typed «nah, that's sus» — «sus» means suspicious — and the player swore at him and left. Jayden felt like a genius. Nobody doubles your stuff for free.
explain: Other scams use excitement or panic. Leo got a message in Minecraft: «You won a free rank! Enter your parents' email and card.» His mum said: «If it needs my card, it isn't free.» Ruby got an email: «Your game account will be DELETED in 24 hours unless you verify here!» It came from a weird address; they deleted it. An app told Mia «Spin the wheel for free gems!» She won — «watch 10 ads to claim». After 10 ads: «Spin again!» She watched 30 ads and got nothing. The app earned money from every ad she watched.
explain: Some tricks come from people you know, or seem to. Noah got «click this link, it's so funny lol» from his friend's account. His friend never says «lol». Noah asked him on another app — the account had been hacked. A YouTube video promised a «secret glitch for unlimited coins, 100% working»; the comments said «it works!» from accounts with no pictures and the same words. Zara wasted an hour. Kai paid a kid at school £5 for a «rare» item that turned out to be common. And Grace's friend said «My cousin can hack you free Robux, just give him your login» — Grace said no, and a month later the same cousin stole the friend's own account. Anyone who can hack for you can hack you.
fact: Real game companies never ask for your password on another website or in chat.
fact: Moderators and game staff never need your password; someone who asks is pretending.
fact: An offer to «double» your coins or items if you send yours first is a trick to keep them.
fact: If something «free» asks for a parent's card, it is not free.
fact: Emails that threaten to delete your account unless you act at once are made to panic you.
fact: Some apps make money from ads, so a prize that always needs «just a few more ads» may never come.
fact: A message from a friend's account that doesn't sound like them may come from a hacker, and its link may steal your account too; check with the friend another way.
fact: Comments saying «it works!» can be fake, written by accounts with no history.
fact: Before you buy or trade an item, check what it is really worth.
fact: A person who offers to hack things for you can also hack you.
fact: Being tricked is not your fault; telling a grown-up quickly helps limit the damage.
fact: A «mod» is a moderator who helps run a game or chat; «sus» is short for suspicious; «lol» means «laughing out loud».
exercise: What did the «free skins» page really want from Sam? | His login, to steal his account | His address, to post him a prize | His age, to choose the right skins | His email, to send him news of prizes
exercise: What happened to Sam's account? | It was taken and sold to a stranger | It got lots of new free skins | It moved to a faster server | It was saved forever by Roblox in a safe place
exercise: Whose shame is it when someone is tricked by a scam? | The scammer's | The person who was tricked | The game company's | Nobody's at all
exercise: A «mod» asks Priya for her password. What is true? | Real mods never ask for passwords | Mods need passwords to check accounts | She should give it if he's polite | She should give half of it
exercise: What is a «mod» in a game? | A moderator who helps run the chat | A special skin you can buy | A cheat that gives you free coins every day | A level that is very hard
exercise: Why was the «double V-bucks» offer a trick? | He'd keep the code and vanish | V-bucks really can be doubled | It was a real Fortnite event | The code would grow by itself
exercise: What does «sus» mean? | Suspicious | Super | Sorry | Sure
exercise: What did Leo's mum say about the «free rank»? | If it needs my card, it isn't free | Free ranks are always real | Enter my email and phone number, but not my card | You must have been lucky
exercise: How did Ruby know the «DELETED in 24 hours» email was a scam? | It used panic and came from a weird address | It was sent on a Tuesday afternoon, when she was at school | It used her real name in it | It had the game's logo on it
exercise: How did the «spin the wheel» app really make money? | From the ads Mia kept watching | From the gems it gave away to players | From selling Mia's phone | From Mia's teacher
exercise: Why did Noah think his friend's message was odd? | His friend never says «lol» | His friend always sends links | It came late at night, after bedtime | It had no spelling mistakes at all
exercise: What did Noah do to check the message? | Asked his friend on another app | Clicked the link to see | Replied «is this you?» to the message | Sent it to everyone
exercise: Why were the «it works!» comments not proof? | They came from fake accounts with the same words | Comments are always true | There were only two of them, both from the same kid | They were written in capitals
exercise: What should Kai have done before paying £5 for the «rare» item? | Checked what it was really worth | Paid £10 to get two of them | Asked the kid to swear on it | Bought it from a younger kid
exercise: Why was the cousin who «hacks free Robux» dangerous? | Anyone who can hack for you can hack you | Hackers always give real Robux to family | He was too young to hack | Robux cannot be hacked at all
exercise: What should you do first if you've been tricked? | Tell a grown-up quickly | Keep it secret forever | Try another free site | Make a new account
test: Rando in chat: «yo im staff, need ur pw to verify, quick». Best reply? | Nothing — block and report | Send it, staff need it to check you | Send it but change it later | Ask him to say please first
test: «send me ur gift card code n I'll send back DOUBLE, no cap». Jayden: «nah, that's sus». Is Jayden right? | Yes, it's a «you first» trick | No, doubling is a real thing | No, «no cap» means it's true | Yes, but only on Mondays
test: Email: «ACCOUNT DELETED IN 24 HRS!!! verify NOW». What's the smart thing? | Slow down and show a grown-up | Click fast before it's deleted | Reply with your password | Make three new accounts
test: Mia: «just 10 more ads and I get the gems». It's her 30th ad. What's going on? | The prize keeps moving; it was never there | She is very close to winning now, only one more ad to go | The app is broken but will pay soon | Gems take exactly 31 ads
test: Best mate's account sends «lol click this its so funny». He never says lol. What would happen if Noah clicked? | His own account might get stolen too | He'd see a funny video, nothing else | His mate would get a prize | The game would give him a rank
test: A video has 1,000 comments saying «IT WORKS!!» What should you look at? | Who's commenting — real accounts or fakes | Only how many comments and likes there are | The colour of the video's thumbnail | How loud the man in the video is
test: Grace's friend: «my cousin's a pro hacker, he'll get u free robux». What's true? | Giving your login to a hacker is giving him your account | Pro hackers are always kind | It's fine if he's someone's cousin, because family can be trusted | Robux from hackers are extra safe
test: Kid at school: «well rare item, a fiver, trust me». What should you do? | Check its real worth before paying | Pay at once before it's gone | Pay double so he's happy | Trust him because he said «trust me»
test: Sam, sobbing: «I'm so thick, I fell for it». What's a true thing to tell him? | It's not your fault — tricksters are clever | Yes, only thick people get tricked | Don't tell anyone, it's too embarrassing to talk about | You'll never be able to play again
test: In a cartoon, a kid clicks «FREE COINS» and gets a mountain of gold. What happens more often in real life? | He loses his account or gets nothing | He gets even more gold than that | He wins the game straight away | The game gives him a medal
test: Leo: «it says I won, just need mum's card for the free rank». Which is right? | Free things don't need a card | Free things always need a card | Use Dad's card instead of Mum's | Only give the first four numbers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person professing to be a moderator requests a user's password. | To obtain unauthorised control of the user's account | To verify the user's account in accordance with policy | To award the user a promotion in rank | To protect the user from other players
exam: It is given that an application requires the viewing of advertisements prior to the award of a prize, and that the requirement is repeatedly renewed. Determine the most probable explanation. | The application derives revenue from the advertisements and the prize is illusory | The prize is awarded after a precise number of advertisements, as stated in the rules | The advertisements are required by law | The application is malfunctioning temporarily
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a message, purportedly from a friend, whose wording is inconsistent with the friend's habits? | It may originate from a person who has compromised the friend's account | It is certainly genuine, the friend's name and picture being displayed above it | It should be acted upon without delay | It constitutes an official notice from the game
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an offer to obtain free currency by unauthorised access to a game. | The person offering may equally obtain unauthorised access to the recipient's account | The offer is lawful provided it is free | The currency so obtained is entirely secure | Such offers are made exclusively by game staff
